{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 3751 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "552620ceefe38482a4ac53a569771e7223bf3f715c1a6e588f94bdc2a5cf7a46" :edb/db-before-t 3750 :edb/db-after-t 3751 :edb/tx-data [[13194139537063 50 #inst "2026-10-18T03:41:49.071Z" 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229636 1071 "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229636 1071 "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.67$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=1.20$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=1.80$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=2.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229636 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.67$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.20$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.80$ falls *within* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.25$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for the values $\\lambda Δx=0.67,$ $\\lambda Δx=1.20,$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.80.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229636 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.67$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.20$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.80$ falls *within* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.25$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for the values $\\lambda Δx=0.67,$ $\\lambda Δx=1.20,$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.80.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\lambda Δx=0.67$; $\\lambda Δx=1.20$; $\\lambda Δx=1.80$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229645 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229645 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.25$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.5$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229645 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.25)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.25}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.25}| \\\\  & =0.8<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & =0.5<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.5)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.5}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.5}| \\\\  & =0.4<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(9)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+9}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{10}| \\\\  & =0.1<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229645 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.25)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.25}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.25}| \\\\  & =0.8<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & =0.5<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.5)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.5}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.5}| \\\\  & =0.4<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(9)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+9}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{10}| \\\\  & =0.1<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\lambda Δx=0.25$; $\\lambda Δx=1$; $\\lambda Δx=1.5$; $\\lambda Δx=9$.\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229652 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-4Δx|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.3.$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229652 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229652 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)((1-4Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-4Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-4Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-4Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-4Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229652 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$” is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)((1-4Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-4Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement “The method is stable for $|1-4Δx|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-4Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-4Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-4Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$” is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.3.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The method is stable for $|1-4Δx|<1.$”; “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229667 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229667 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 1$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229667 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=x{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂[1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229667 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=x{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂[1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$; ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 1$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229680 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229680 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229680 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=2tx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(tx)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in R.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t<0$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ is positive, so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229680 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=2tx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(tx)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in R.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t<0$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ is positive, so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$; ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$; ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229707 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186229707 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n- **I.** ${D}_{1}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- **II.** ${D}_{2}(y)={y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- **III.** ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229707 1074 "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=-2,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is ${y}^{2},$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The last term contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{2},$ which fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186229707 1074 "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=-2,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is ${y}^{2},$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The last term contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{2},$ which fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${D}_{1}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186233061 1071 "![](images/d9/d9cf9a5c61cbf1796cf6682b129b4c05171c364c7b34e7cce176ba27e9c9cb24.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186233061 1071 "![](images/d9/d9cf9a5c61cbf1796cf6682b129b4c05171c364c7b34e7cce176ba27e9c9cb24.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-1$\n- **II.** $x=2$\n- **III.** $x=4$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186233061 1074 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=4.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=4,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and II only\".\n\n![](images/95/95967eb82512aae261936a6c09e2f474c72c9a130d3ecb527f62408822d88c96.png)" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186233061 1074 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=4.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=4,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  $x=-1$; $x=2$ only.\n\n![](images/95/95967eb82512aae261936a6c09e2f474c72c9a130d3ecb527f62408822d88c96.png)" 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186233151 1071 "![](images/7b/7b0ca55d089bea13471e9de6dd26093a1a9d477937106c92be26d5a469778c8a.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186233151 1071 "![](images/7b/7b0ca55d089bea13471e9de6dd26093a1a9d477937106c92be26d5a469778c8a.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-2$ is a simple root.\n- **II.** $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\n- **III.** $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186233151 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the \n$x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=2$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186233151 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$x=-2$ is a simple root” is true. The root $x=-2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the \n$x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement “$x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity” is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement “$x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity” is true. The root $x=2$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$x=-2$ is a simple root”; “$x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity”; “$x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity”." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186233253 1071 "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186233253 1071 "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $9x{y}^{''}+8{y}^{'}=4$\n- **II.** $\\sqrt{{y}^{''}+x}=3y$\n- **III.** ${y}^{‴}+{x}^{2}{y}^{''}=3$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186233253 1074 "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation $9x{y}^{''}+8{y}^{'}=4$ is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\n- Equation $\\sqrt{{y}^{''}+x}=3y$ is also a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{‴}+{x}^{2}{y}^{''}=3$ is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $9x{y}^{''}+8{y}^{'}=4$; $\\sqrt{{y}^{''}+x}=3y$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186233253 1074 "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\n- Equation II is also a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186233256 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186233256 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{'}-8{e}^{y}=2$\n- **II.** $y{y}^{'}+3=\\operatorname{cos}y$\n- **III.** $x{y}^{'}-8={e}^{y}$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186233256 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation ${y}^{'}-8{e}^{y}=2$ is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-8{e}^{y} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =8{e}^{y}+2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=8{e}^{y}+2.$\n\n- Equation $y{y}^{'}+3=\\operatorname{cos}y$ is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{'}+3 & =\\operatorname{cos}y \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation $x{y}^{'}-8={e}^{y}$ is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-8 & ={e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{'}-8{e}^{y}=2$; $y{y}^{'}+3=\\operatorname{cos}y$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186233256 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-8{e}^{y} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =8{e}^{y}+2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=8{e}^{y}+2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{'}+3 & =\\operatorname{cos}y \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-8 & ={e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186233376 1071 "Which of the following is a $3\\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186233376 1071 "Which of the following is a $3\\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nE = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nF = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nG = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186233427 1071 "![](images/bc/bc3809e55e601738d56be6885475ed442dec1fb3a0d2e19da14be045510f73dc.png)\n\nThe above scatter plot shows the relationship between age and years of experience of a group of professional soccer players. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Only one player has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n\nII. There are two players aged $21.$\n\nIII. None of the players has fewer than $6$ years of experience.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186233427 1071 "![](images/bc/bc3809e55e601738d56be6885475ed442dec1fb3a0d2e19da14be045510f73dc.png)\n\nThe above scatter plot shows the relationship between age and years of experience of a group of professional soccer players. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186233427 1074 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $10,$ namely, $(21,10).$ This means only one of the players has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n\n![](images/20/209759f5d70fd8587740d1da840f9da838a3c9a055a0d1e962f76c1cd6341fd3.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $21,$ namely, $(21