f86a064753.png)" 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186237873 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-2, & x<0 \\\\ -2, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186237873 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-2, & x<0 \\\\ -2, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It is continuous at $x=0$\n- **II.** It is differentiable at $x=0$\n- **III.** It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186237873 1074 "- Statement I is true  Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, at  $x=0.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x-2)=2(0)-2=-2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}-2=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=f(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ .\n\n- Statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\,$  if  $\\,x<0$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>0$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$   Since $x=0$  doesn't belong to $(0,2)$, the given function is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186237873 1074 "- Statement “It is continuous at $x=0$” is true  Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, at  $x=0.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x-2)=2(0)-2=-2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}-2=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=f(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ .\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable at $x=0$” is false, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\,$  if  $\\,x<0$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>0$\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$” is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$   Since $x=0$  doesn't belong to $(0,2)$, the given function is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, only statements “It is continuous at $x=0$” and “It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$” are true." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186237917 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n\nII. $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n\nIII. $C={A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186237917 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186237917 1074 "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA