hrough each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is defined everywhere, except where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186237987 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/73/732955302ca49a41f6c43cf242d7c869e630e88d804c4b56a5beb3b081f2d230.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The domain of the function is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $” is true. The function is defined everywhere, except where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “The function has zeros at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n$” is false. The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$” is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The domain of the function is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186237989 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186237989 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The vertical asymptotes of the function are $x=2n\\pi $\n- **II.** Its graph passes through the point $(0,0)$\n- **III.** $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186237989 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2d0606829588b01f6cba2fa4785b201c47a6d9ffa325d54e64544c9769d7e4a.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has an asymptote at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and the period is $\\pi .$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the graph of the function passes through the point $(0,0).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186237989 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2d0606829588b01f6cba2fa4785b201c47a6d9ffa325d54e64544c9769d7e4a.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement “The vertical asymptotes of the function are $x=2n\\pi $” is false. The function has an asymptote at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and the period is $\\pi .$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “Its graph passes through the point $(0,0)$” is true. Indeed, the graph of the function passes through the point $(0,0).$\n\n- Statement $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “Its graph passes through the point $(0,0)$”; $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186238005 1071 "![](images/fb/fb61c048eb7eac87f82c53717f8f67251b59610817ddf1f267e80543c0c86959.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-1$\n\nII. $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=-1$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186238005 1071 "![](images/fb/fb61c048eb7eac87f82c53717f8f67251b59610817ddf1f267e80543c0c86959.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186238005 1074 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=-1,$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a critical point at $x=-1$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=-1$ since $f(-1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186238005 1074 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-1$” is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=-1,$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$” is true. The function has a critical point at $x=-1$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=-1$” is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=-1$ since $f(-1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, statements “$f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-1$” and “$f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$” are true, while statement “$f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=-1$” is false." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186238006 1071 "![](images/5b/5bbb0cacbe8dca2facfd49e757ba9b7aa40ee91ae820f001c72d637186d57b79.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=1