main of $g$ is $R$” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240968 1071 "![](images/8f/8ff01afa1993f8e301edb69e3e7a13ee71ab01b1a3da416aa53ee94d021184e1.png)\n\nConsider the function $g:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$\n- The codomain of $g$ is ${-1,0,1,2}$\n- The range of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240968 1071 "![](images/8f/8ff01afa1993f8e301edb69e3e7a13ee71ab01b1a3da416aa53ee94d021184e1.png)\n\nConsider the function $g:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240968 1074 "The notation $g:X\\to Y$ means that the function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $g$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X={-1,0,1,2},$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y={0,1,2},$\n\n- the range of $g$ is ${0,1,2},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"III only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240968 1074 "The notation $g:X\\to Y$ means that the function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $g$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X={-1,0,1,2},$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y={0,1,2},$\n\n- the range of $g$ is ${0,1,2},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements “The domain of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$” and “The codomain of $g$ is ${-1,0,1,2}$” are false, while statement “The range of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$” is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer “The range of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240969 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{x}^{2}-1) & 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & {e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{y}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240969 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{x}^{2}-1) & 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & {e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{y}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(0,0)>0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0$\n- **III.** The point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240969 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $H(0,0)>0$ is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0$ is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement “The point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$” is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $H(0,0)>0$; “The point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240969 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240970 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240970 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(1,1)>0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n- **III.** The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240970 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-3y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3y)=6x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $H(1,1)>0$ is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))={6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}=27>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$ is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=6>0.$\n\n- Statement “The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$” is false. Since $H(1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $H(1,1)>0$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240970 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-3y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3y)=6x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))={6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}=27>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=6>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240972 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}_{0}^{-}$ (the set of non-positive rational numbers)?\n\nI. $Z$\n\nII. ${Z}^{-}$ (the set of negative integers)\n\nIII. $N$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240972 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}_{0}^{-}$ (the set of non-positive rational numbers)?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240972 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $Z$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in Z$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\n- ${Z}^{-}$ is  a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ Every negative integer is a non-positive rational number.\n\n- $N$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in N$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240972 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $Z$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in Z$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\n- ${Z}^{-}$ is  a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ Every negative integer is a non-positive rational number.\n\n- $N$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in N$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${Z}^{-}$ (the set of negative integers)” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240973 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240973 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of $R?$\n- **I.** ${Q}^{-}$ (the set of negative rationals)\n- **II.** ${Z}^{+}$ (the set of positive integers)\n- **III.** $N$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240973 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ is  a subset of $R.$ Every negative rational number is a real number.\n\n- ${Z}^{+}$ is a subset of $R.$ Every positive integer number is a real number.\n\n- $N$ a subset of $R.$ Every natural number is a real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240973 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ is  a subset of $R.$ Every negative rational number is a real number.\n\n- ${Z}^{+}$ is a subset of $R.$ Every positive integer number is a real number.\n\n- $N$ a subset of $R.$ Every natural number is a real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${Q}^{-}$ (the set of negative rationals)”; “${Z}^{+}$ (the set of positive integers)”; “$N$ (natural numbers)”." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240974 1071 "![](images/0b/0bc0e75bb577ca37b89568e14a4e0d03da7ce4f4853c4e8c57d9b2df79897b13.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240974 1071 "![](images/0b/0bc0e75bb577ca37b89568e14a4e0d03da7ce4f4853c4e8c57d9b2df79897b13.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- **II.** $∇\\cdot F>0$\n- **III.** $∇\\cdot F<0$\n- **IV.** $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240974 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $∇\\cdot F=0$ is true, whereas statements $∇\\cdot F>0$ and $∇\\cdot F<0$ are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is solenoidal” is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $∇\\cdot F=0$; “$F$ is solenoidal” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240974 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240975 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in N∣n-2\\ge 4}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n+2\\ge 5}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240975 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in N∣n-2\\ge 4}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n+2\\ge 5}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A⊆B$\n- **II.** $B⊆A$\n- **III.** $∅⊆A$\n- **IV.** $B⊇A$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240975 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge 4,$ that is to say, $n\\ge 6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={6,7,8,9,10,11,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\ge 5,$ That is to say, $n\\ge 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={3,4,5,6,7,8,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $3\\in B$ but $3\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆A$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240975 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge 4,$ that is to say, $n\\ge 6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={6,7,8,9,10,11,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\ge 5,$ That is to say, $n\\ge 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={3,4,5,6,7,8,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $3\\in B$ but $3\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆A$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A⊆B$; $∅⊆A$; $B⊇A$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240976 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z∣x\\le 5}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣-7\\le x<3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186240976 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z∣x\\le 5}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣-7\\le x<3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A⊆B$\n- **II.** $B⊆A$\n- **III.** $B⊈A$\n- **IV.** $A⊇B$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240976 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $x\\le 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $-7\\le x<3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={-7,-6,-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false.  For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186240976 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $x\\le 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $-7\\le x<3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={-7,-6,-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false.  For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $B⊆A$; $A⊇B$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186241002 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nI. $[4,8]\\cup [10,12]$\n\nII. $[0,5)$\n\nIII. $[1,3)\\cup (3,7]$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186241002 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186241002 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $[4,8]\\cup [10,12]$ is closed. It consists of two parts, where the boundary points are $4,8,10,$ and $12.$ All of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not closed because:\n\n- $[0,5)$ has a boundary point $5$, which does not lie in the set.\n\n- $[1,3)\\cup (3,7]$ has a boundary point $3$, which does not lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186241002 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $[4,8]\\cup [10,12]$ is closed. It consists of two parts, where the boundary points are $4,8,10,$ and $12.$ All of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not closed because:\n\n- $[0,5)$ has a boundary point $5$, which does not lie in the set.\n\n- $[1,3)\\cup (3,7]$ has a boundary point $3$, which does not lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[4,8]\\cup [10,12]$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186241009 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186241009 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n- **I.** $(2,5)$\n- **II.** $(-3,0)\\cup (2,7)$\n- **III.** $(-2,2)\\cup (4,10]$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186241009 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $(2,5)$ and $(-3,0)\\cup (2,7)$ are open because:\n\n- The boundary points of the set $(2,5)$ are $2$, and $5.$ None of them lie in the set.\n\n- The boundary points of the set  $(-3,0)\\cup (2,7)$ are $-3$, $0$, $2$, and $7.$ None of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other set is not open because $(-2,2)\\cup (4,10]$ has four boundary points $-2$, $2$, $4$, and $10$, and the point $10$ lies in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186241009 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $(2,5)$ and $(-3,0)\\cup (2,7)$ are open because:\n\n- The boundary points of the set $(2,5)$ are $2$, and $5.$ None of them lie in the set.\n\n- The boundary points of the set  $(-3,0)\\cup (2,7)$ are $-3$, $0$, $2$, and $7.$ None of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other set is not open because $(-2,2)\\cup (4,10]$ has four boundary points $-2$, $2$, $4$, and $10$, and the point $10$ lies in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(2,5)$; $(-3,0)\\cup (2,7)$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186241031 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- **II.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\n- **III.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186241031 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186241031 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}+8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-16{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186241031 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}+8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-16{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements “The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$” and “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent” are false, while statement “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent” is true." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186241033 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186241033 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a