 turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=0.$ Hence, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 0=0,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any matrices $A,B\\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)-C=(A+B)+(-C)=A+(B+(-C))=A+(B-C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186243917 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “The  zero-matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $L.$” is true. Indeed, $\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=0.$ Hence, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “For any $A\\in L,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in L$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$” is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 0=0,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “For any $A,B,C\\in L,$ we have $(A+B)-C=A+(B-C).$” is true. Indeed, for any matrices $A,B\\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)-C=(A+B)+(-C)=A+(B+(-C))=A+(B-C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The  zero-matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $L.$”; “For any $A\\in L,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in L$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$”; “For any $A,B,C\\in L,$ we have $(A+B)-C=A+(B-C).$”." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186243920 1071 "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.