 S,$ we have\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The identity matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $S.$”; “For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186243926 1071 "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}a & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{bmatrix},$ where $a,b,c\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186243926 1071 "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}a & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{bmatrix},$ where $a,b,c\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** For any $A\\in M,$ we have $-A\\in M$ such that $A+(-A)=0.$\n- **II.** For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in M,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- **III.** For any $A,B,C\\in M,$ we have $(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186243926 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in M.$ Then $-A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the corresponding negative matrix such that $A+(-A)=O.$ However, $-{a}_{12}=-1\\ne 1,$ hence, $-A\\notin M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-matrix $O$ and any matrix $A\\in M,$ we have\n\n$$\nA+O=A\n$$\n\nsince $O$ is the unique zero element in the set of all matrices.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B,C\\in M,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C)\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186243926 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “For any $A\\in M,$ we have $-A\\in M$ such that $A+(-A)=0.$” is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in M.$ Then $-A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the corresponding negative matrix such that $A+(-A)=O.$ However, $-{a}_{12}=-1\\ne 1,$ hence, $-A\\notin M.$\n\n- Statement “For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in M,$ we have $A+O=A.$” is true. Indeed, for the zero-matrix $O$ and any matrix $A\\in M,$ we have\n\n$$\nA+O=A\n$$\n\nsince $O$ is the unique zero element in the set of all matrices.\n\n- Statement “For any $A,B,C\\in M,$ we have $(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).$” is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B,C\\in M,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C)\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in M,$ we have $A+O=A.$”; “For any $A,B,C\\in M,$ we have $(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).$” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186243929 1071 "Let $T$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $10.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186243929 1071 "Let $T$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $10.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** For the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in T,$ we have $p(x)+0=0.$\n- **II.** For any $p(x),q(x)\\in T$, we have $p(x)+q(x)=p(x)-q(x).$\n- **III.** For any polynomial $p(x)\\in T$ we have $-p(x)\\in T$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186243929 1074 "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}$ in $T.$ Then,\n\n$$\np(x)+0={x}^{2}+0={x}^{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=q(x)={x}^{2}\\in T.$\nThen:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x)+q(x) & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ p(x)-q(x) & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\ne p(x)-q(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x)\\in T,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x)+(-p(x))=p(x)-p(x)=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(-p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 10,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x)\\in T.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186243929 1074 "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement “For the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in T,$ we have $p(x)+0=0.$” is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}$ in $T.$ Then,\n\n$$\np(x)+0={x}^{2}+0={x}^{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “For any $p(x),q(x)\\in T$, we have $p(x)+q(x)=p(x)-q(x).$” is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=q(x)={x}^{2}\\in T.$\nThen:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x)+q(x) & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ p(x)-q(x) & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\ne p(x)-q(x).$\n\n- Statement “For any polynomial $p(x)\\in T$ we have $-p(x)\\in T$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$” is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x)\\in T,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x)+(-p(x))=p(x)-p(x)=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(-p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 10,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x)\\in T.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “For any polynomial $p(x)\\in T$ we have $-p(x)\\in T$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186243932 1071 "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials $p(x)$ such that $p(0)<0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186243932 1071 "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials $p(x)$ such that $p(0)<0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The zero-polynomial belongs to $C.$\n- **II.** For any $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n- **III.** The addition of polynomials in $C$ is associative." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186243932 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials that evaluate to a negative number at $0.$ However, the zero-polynomial is *not* such a polynomial, as it equals $0≮0$ at $0.$ So, $0\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186243932 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement “The zero-polynomial belongs to $C.$” is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials that evaluate to a negative number at $0.$ However, the zero-polynomial is *not* such a polynomial, as it equals $0≮0$ at $0.$ So, $0\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement “For any $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$” is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement “The addition of polynomials in $C$ is associative” is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “For any $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$”; “The addition of polynomials in $C$ is associative” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186243945 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186243945 1071 "Which of the following product