rix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247105 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247106 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247106 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247160 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247160 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n- **I.** $A={⟨2t,2+t⟩\\,:\\,t\\in R}$\n- **II.** $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0}$\n- **III.** $C={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247160 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have, for some $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 2+t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t &=0 \\\\ 2+t &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe see that the system has no solution. So, $0$ is not in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace.  It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $y=1$ since $0\\ne 1.$ So, $0$ is not in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247160 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have, for some $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 2+t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t &=0 \\\\ 2+t &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe see that the system has no solution. So, $0$ is not in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace.  It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $y=1$ since $0\\ne 1.$ So, $0$ is not in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0}$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247218 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $4,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247218 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $4,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n- **II.** $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $3$\n- **III.** $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247218 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{4},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ that could cancel the ${x}^{4}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P-Q$  still contains ${x}^{4}.$  Therefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must always be $4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the cases where $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is a polynomial, we need that the degree of $P$ should be greater or equal to the degree of $Q,$ which is not the case.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247218 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$P+Q$ is always a polynomial” is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement “$P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $3$” is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{4},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ that could cancel the ${x}^{4}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P-Q$  still contains ${x}^{4}.$  Therefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must always be $4.$\n\n- Statement “$\\frac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial” is false. For the cases where $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is a polynomial, we need that the degree of $P$ should be greater or equal to the degree of $Q,$ which is not the case.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “$P+Q$ is always a polynomial” is true." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247219 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247219 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $4$\n- **II.** $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n- **III.** $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $2$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247219 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{2+2}={x}^{4},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{2}+x$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{2}+x-{x}^{2}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q=-{x}^{2}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247219 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $4$” is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{2+2}={x}^{4},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $4.$\n\n- Statement “$P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$” is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{2}+x$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{2}+x-{x}^{2}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement “$P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $2$” is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q=-{x}^{2}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements “$P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $4$” and “$P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$” are true." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247243 1071 "![](images/b4/b46093feaf5d7138e30c6d6fc673a2d915cac74a23ae76e0841e9bb5b135fa60.png)\n\nThe graph of the polynomial $y=P(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The degree of $P(x)$ is even.\n- The coefficient of the leading term is positive.\n- The constant term is positive.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247243 1071 "![](images/b4/b46093fe