formed into the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247254 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247254 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247254 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I:$\n\n$$\nA-4I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247254 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I:$\n\n$$\nA-4I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is “$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$” only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247261 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186247261 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247289 1071 "![](images/5e/5e4e2241490626139e15800f3b90c8e15a9963bdcace9907f8557b0897d533e6.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186247289 1071 "![](images/5e/5e4e22414