iven integrals.\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$ is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$ is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}$ is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}$ only." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186250353 1074 "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186250375 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 true] [17592186250375 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537063 17592186576411 false] [17592186250375 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd functio